Each vertex has 4 possible diagonal endpoints (exclude itself and its two neighbors). Counting each diagonal from both ends gives \(7\cdot4/2=14\) diagonals, so there are \(\binom{14}{2}=91\) possible pairs.
An interior crossing uses four distinct vertices. Conversely, any four vertices form a convex quadrilateral, and its two diagonals are the unique crossing pair on those vertices. Thus there are \(\binom74=35\) crossing pairs, giving probability \(35/91=5/13\).
Final answer: \dfrac{5}{13}
